# Men's Princess Cut Ring: 3 Corner Risks Over $4K

[![Men's princess cut ring in white gold structured solitaire — 3 corner risks and V-prong protection guide](/images/mens-princess-cut-ring-featured.jpg "Men's Princess Cut Ring: 3 Corner Risks Over $4K — DiamondCritics")](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?Genders=men&ShapeID=Princess&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=Image)

## TL;DR — Men's Princess Cut Ring in One Scroll

**Princess cut is the only popular diamond shape with four 90-degree pointed corners. Those corners chip. Without V-prong protection, a $2,000+ stone becomes scrap. Here are the 3 risks and the picks that eliminate them.**

1. **Risk 1 — Standard Prongs Miss the Corner:** Round-head prongs seat between corners, not over them. The corner tip sits exposed. One hard tap on a countertop chips it.
2. **Risk 2 — Active-Wear Corner Exposure:** Men's bands rotate. A princess corner on the side of a 7mm band hits surfaces that a women's setting never encounters. Without V-prong, that corner is unprotected 360°.
3. **Risk 3 — Width-Prong Gap:** When the princess stone is too small for the band width, the prong geometry creates a gap at the corner. The stone looks like it's floating — and the exposed corner amplifies chip risk.

**All solitaire settings in this guide are setting only — add your princess stone with V-prong heads. The Corner Formula tells you exactly what size stone fits each band.**

