## V-Prong vs Standard Prong — Princess on a Men's Band

| Prong Type | Corner Coverage | Men's Band Risk | Maintenance | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Standard Round-Head | None — seats at girdle edge, not corner | High — corner exposed 360° on rotating band | Re-tip every 5–10 yrs | Wrong prong for princess cut; corner unprotected |
| V-Prong (correct) | Full — folds over 90° corner tip | Low — 0.3mm metal overhang absorbs impact | Re-tip every 5–10 yrs | Required for princess; specify to your diamond setter |
| Bezel (not available off-shelf) | Complete — metal ring encircles stone | None | None | Maximum protection; requires custom work |

**Verdict:** Standard round-head prongs are incompatible with princess cut on a daily-wear men's ring. V-prong is not optional — it is the minimum protective standard. When ordering a solitaire setting, tell your diamond setter: "V-prong heads on all four corners, 0.3mm overhang past the corner tip." If the bench cannot confirm this, find a different setter.

