## Expert Summary

**Farzana Hasan, diamond expert** | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

Princess cut makes geometric sense on a men's band. The square stone fits the rectangular band architecture in a way a round stone does not. The 35% price advantage over round at equivalent certification is real and consistent. Use that savings to upgrade the setting metal or stage the purchase.

The corner risk is not marketing language. It is a physical reality. Princess cut corners are ground to 90 degrees. That angle concentrates impact force at the thinnest part of the stone. On a rotating men's band, that corner faces every direction under daily wear. V-prong heads with 0.3mm overhang are the solution. Without them, a $2,000+ stone is a liability.

Pick the stone first from certified inventory. Then match the band width using the Corner Formula. Then confirm V-prong heads with your setter before the stone is placed.

Entry point: [$3,365](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?Genders=men&ShapeID=Princess&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a GIA-certified 1ct princess on a men's ridged solitaire. Lab-grown 1.5ct option: [$4,712](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417594?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

